// High school intro physics quizzes — hp01 through hp18
export const HS_PHYSICS_QUIZZES = [
  { id:'hp01', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Newton's Laws of Motion`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`Newton's First Law states that an object at rest will:`, ch:[`Always start moving eventually`,`Stay at rest unless acted on by an outside force`,`Accelerate on its own`,`Lose mass over time`], ans:1, exp:`Newton's First Law (the law of inertia) says objects stay at rest, or keep moving at a constant velocity, unless a net force acts on them.` },
      { q:`Which formula represents Newton's Second Law?`, ch:[`F = ma`,`E = mc squared`,`V = IR`,`P = mv`], ans:0, exp:`Newton's Second Law states that force equals mass times acceleration (F = ma).` },
      { q:`Newton's Third Law says that for every action there is:`, ch:[`No reaction at all`,`A smaller reaction`,`An equal and opposite reaction`,`A delayed reaction`], ans:2, exp:`Newton's Third Law states that forces always come in pairs — for every action force, there is an equal and opposite reaction force.` },
      { q:`What is the SI unit of force?`, ch:[`Joule`,`Watt`,`Newton`,`Pascal`], ans:2, exp:`Force is measured in newtons (N), named after Isaac Newton. One newton is the force needed to accelerate 1 kg at 1 m/s squared.` },
      { q:`An object moving at a constant velocity in a straight line will keep doing so unless:`, ch:[`It runs out of energy`,`A net outside force acts on it`,`Too much time passes`,`It reaches the speed of light`], ans:1, exp:`This is Newton's First Law applied to a moving object — it keeps its velocity unless a net force changes it.` },
      { q:`Which of these objects has the greatest inertia?`, ch:[`A tennis ball`,`A feather`,`A bowling ball`,`A balloon`], ans:2, exp:`Inertia depends on mass — the more mass an object has, the more it resists changes in motion. The bowling ball has the most mass here.` },
      { q:`If you double the force applied to an object while its mass stays the same, its acceleration will:`, ch:[`Stay the same`,`Double`,`Be cut in half`,`Quadruple`], ans:1, exp:`Since F = ma, acceleration is directly proportional to force when mass is constant, so doubling the force doubles the acceleration.` },
      { q:`If the mass of an object doubles while the force applied to it stays the same, its acceleration will:`, ch:[`Double`,`Stay the same`,`Be cut in half`,`Quadruple`], ans:2, exp:`Since a = F/m, acceleration is inversely proportional to mass. Doubling the mass cuts the acceleration in half.` },
      { q:`A rocket blasts burning gas out of its rear nozzle and speeds forward. This best demonstrates:`, ch:[`Newton's First Law`,`Newton's Second Law`,`Newton's Third Law`,`The law of conservation of mass`], ans:2, exp:`The rocket pushes gas backward (action), and the gas pushes the rocket forward (reaction) — a classic example of Newton's Third Law.` },
      { q:`A 2 kg cart is pushed with a net force that gives it an acceleration of 3 m/s squared. What is the force?`, ch:[`5 N`,`6 N`,`1.5 N`,`9 N`], ans:1, exp:`Using F = ma: F = 2 kg times 3 m/s squared = 6 N.` },
      { q:`Which of the following is NOT one of Newton's Three Laws of Motion?`, ch:[`An object in motion stays in motion unless acted on by a force`,`Force equals mass times acceleration`,`For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction`,`Energy cannot be created or destroyed`], ans:3, exp:`Energy cannot be created or destroyed is the law of conservation of energy, not one of Newton's three laws of motion.` },
      { q:`Why do passengers lurch forward when a car stops suddenly?`, ch:[`The car pushes them forward`,`Their bodies keep moving forward due to inertia`,`Gravity pulls them forward`,`Air pressure increases inside the car`], ans:1, exp:`A passenger's body wants to keep moving at its original speed (inertia) even after the car stops, which is why seatbelts are needed.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp02', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Speed, Velocity & Acceleration`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`What is the main difference between speed and velocity?`, ch:[`They are exactly the same thing`,`Velocity includes direction, speed does not`,`Speed includes direction, velocity does not`,`Velocity can only be measured in space`], ans:1, exp:`Speed is just how fast something moves, while velocity also includes the direction of motion.` },
      { q:`Average speed is calculated as:`, ch:[`Distance divided by time`,`Time divided by distance`,`Distance times time`,`Mass divided by time`], ans:0, exp:`Average speed equals the total distance traveled divided by the total time taken.` },
      { q:`A car travels 100 miles in 2 hours. What is its average speed?`, ch:[`200 mph`,`50 mph`,`102 mph`,`25 mph`], ans:1, exp:`Average speed = distance / time = 100 miles / 2 hours = 50 mph.` },
      { q:`Acceleration is best described as the rate of change of:`, ch:[`Distance`,`Mass`,`Velocity`,`Time`], ans:2, exp:`Acceleration measures how quickly velocity changes, whether in speed, direction, or both.` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for acceleration?`, ch:[`Meters per second`,`Meters per second squared`,`Meters`,`Seconds per meter`], ans:1, exp:`Acceleration has units of meters per second squared (m/s squared) because it is a change in velocity (m/s) per unit time (s).` },
      { q:`A car that is speeding up is experiencing:`, ch:[`Negative acceleration`,`Zero acceleration`,`Positive acceleration`,`Constant velocity`], ans:2, exp:`When an object's speed increases in the direction it is moving, it has positive acceleration.` },
      { q:`A car that is slowing down is often said to be undergoing:`, ch:[`Deceleration`,`Zero velocity`,`Constant speed`,`Positive momentum only`], ans:0, exp:`Deceleration describes acceleration that acts opposite to the direction of motion, causing the object to slow down.` },
      { q:`Velocity is called a vector quantity because it has:`, ch:[`Only magnitude`,`Only direction`,`Both magnitude and direction`,`Neither magnitude nor direction`], ans:2, exp:`Vector quantities like velocity have both a size (magnitude) and a direction, unlike scalar quantities such as speed.` },
      { q:`A runner completes one full lap around a circular track and returns to the starting point. What is their average velocity for the lap?`, ch:[`Equal to their average speed`,`Zero, since displacement is zero`,`Negative`,`Impossible to determine`], ans:1, exp:`Since the runner ends up back at the start, their displacement (straight-line change in position) is zero, so average velocity is zero even though they ran a nonzero distance.` },
      { q:`Which formula correctly calculates acceleration?`, ch:[`(final velocity minus initial velocity) divided by time`,`Final velocity times time`,`Distance divided by time`,`Mass times velocity`], ans:0, exp:`Acceleration equals the change in velocity divided by the time it took for that change to happen.` },
      { q:`A skateboarder speeds up from 0 to 20 m/s in 4 seconds. What is the acceleration?`, ch:[`80 m/s squared`,`0.2 m/s squared`,`5 m/s squared`,`16 m/s squared`], ans:2, exp:`Acceleration = (20 m/s - 0 m/s) / 4 s = 5 m/s squared.` },
      { q:`What is the difference between distance and displacement?`, ch:[`They are always identical`,`Distance is total path length; displacement is straight-line change in position`,`Displacement is always longer than distance`,`Distance includes direction, displacement does not`], ans:1, exp:`Distance is the total length of the path traveled, while displacement is the straight-line distance and direction from the start point to the end point.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp03', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Forces & Friction`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`Friction is a force that:`, ch:[`Speeds up moving objects`,`Opposes motion between two surfaces in contact`,`Only exists in outer space`,`Increases an object's mass`], ans:1, exp:`Friction is a force that resists the relative motion of two surfaces that are touching each other.` },
      { q:`Which surface generally produces more friction?`, ch:[`A smooth, polished surface`,`A rough, bumpy surface`,`Friction is the same on all surfaces`,`Friction depends only on color`], ans:1, exp:`Rougher surfaces have more microscopic bumps that catch on each other, producing more friction than smooth surfaces.` },
      { q:`Static friction acts on an object that is:`, ch:[`Already sliding`,`Falling through the air`,`At rest and resisting the start of motion`,`Floating in water`], ans:2, exp:`Static friction keeps a stationary object from starting to move until enough force is applied to overcome it.` },
      { q:`The friction that acts on an object already sliding across a surface is called:`, ch:[`Static friction`,`Kinetic friction`,`Gravitational friction`,`Magnetic friction`], ans:1, exp:`Kinetic (or sliding) friction opposes the motion of an object that is already moving relative to a surface.` },
      { q:`Which of the following would most likely reduce friction between two surfaces?`, ch:[`Adding sandpaper`,`Pressing the surfaces together harder`,`Adding oil or lubricant`,`Increasing the roughness of both surfaces`], ans:2, exp:`Lubricants like oil create a slippery layer between surfaces, reducing the friction between them.` },
      { q:`Air resistance, which slows down a falling skydiver, is an example of:`, ch:[`Gravity`,`A type of friction from air`,`Magnetism`,`Buoyant force`], ans:1, exp:`Air resistance is a form of friction caused by air molecules pushing against a moving object.` },
      { q:`The normal force is best described as:`, ch:[`A force that always points downward`,`A support force that pushes perpendicular to a surface`,`Another name for gravity`,`A force that only occurs in liquids`], ans:1, exp:`The normal force is the support force a surface exerts on an object resting on it, acting perpendicular (at a right angle) to that surface.` },
      { q:`An object's weight is a force caused by:`, ch:[`Friction from the ground`,`Gravity acting on its mass`,`Air pressure`,`Magnetic attraction`], ans:1, exp:`Weight is the force of gravity pulling on an object's mass, which is why weight changes on different planets while mass stays the same.` },
      { q:`On a table, a book stays at rest because friction between the book and a slightly tilted table surface is:`, ch:[`Zero`,`Large enough to balance the force pulling the book downhill`,`Pushing the book off the table`,`Not a real force`], ans:1, exp:`Static friction can balance other forces acting on a stationary object, keeping it from sliding as long as it is strong enough.` },
      { q:`According to simple friction models, increasing the surface area of contact between two objects:`, ch:[`Always doubles the friction force`,`Does not significantly change the friction force`,`Eliminates friction entirely`,`Always cuts friction in half`], ans:1, exp:`In the basic model of friction, the friction force mainly depends on the surfaces and how hard they press together, not on the contact area.` },
      { q:`Rolling friction (like a ball rolling on the ground) is generally:`, ch:[`Greater than sliding friction`,`Less than sliding friction`,`Exactly equal to sliding friction`,`Not a real type of friction`], ans:1, exp:`Rolling friction is usually much smaller than sliding friction, which is why wheels make it easier to move heavy objects.` },
      { q:`A box is pushed to the right with a 10 N force while friction pushes back on it with 4 N. What is the net force on the box?`, ch:[`14 N to the right`,`6 N to the right`,`4 N to the left`,`0 N`], ans:1, exp:`Net force = 10 N - 4 N = 6 N in the direction of the push (to the right), since friction acts opposite to the motion.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp04', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Work, Energy & Power Basics`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`In physics, work is done when:`, ch:[`A force causes an object to move in the direction of the force`,`You think hard about a problem`,`An object simply exists`,`Time passes`], ans:0, exp:`Work is done only when a force causes an object to move some distance in the direction of that force.` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for work?`, ch:[`Watts`,`Newtons`,`Joules`,`Meters`], ans:2, exp:`Work is measured in joules (J), the same unit used for energy, since work is a transfer of energy.` },
      { q:`If you push hard against a wall and it does not move at all, how much work have you done on the wall?`, ch:[`A large amount`,`Zero`,`A negative amount`,`It depends on how tired you are`], ans:1, exp:`Since the wall does not move, there is no displacement, so no work is done on it even though you applied a force.` },
      { q:`Power is defined as:`, ch:[`Force divided by distance`,`Work divided by time`,`Mass times acceleration`,`Distance divided by mass`], ans:1, exp:`Power measures how quickly work is done, calculated as the amount of work divided by the time it takes.` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for power?`, ch:[`Joules`,`Newtons`,`Watts`,`Meters per second`], ans:2, exp:`Power is measured in watts (W), where one watt equals one joule of work done per second.` },
      { q:`A force of 10 N moves a box 5 meters in the direction of the force. How much work was done?`, ch:[`2 J`,`15 J`,`50 J`,`0.5 J`], ans:2, exp:`Work = force times distance = 10 N times 5 m = 50 J.` },
      { q:`If 100 J of work is done in 5 seconds, what is the power?`, ch:[`500 W`,`20 W`,`105 W`,`95 W`], ans:1, exp:`Power = work / time = 100 J / 5 s = 20 W.` },
      { q:`Lifting a box quickly to a shelf versus lifting the same box slowly to the same shelf:`, ch:[`Requires the same work but more power when done quickly`,`Requires more work when done quickly`,`Requires less work when done quickly`,`Uses no energy either way`], ans:0, exp:`The work done (force times distance) is the same either way, but lifting it faster means doing that work in less time, which requires more power.` },
      { q:`Energy is best defined as:`, ch:[`The ability to do work`,`A type of force`,`The mass of an object`,`The speed of an object`], ans:0, exp:`Energy is the capacity to do work — objects or systems with more energy can exert forces that cause things to move.` },
      { q:`Two machines do the same amount of work, but Machine A finishes in less time than Machine B. Machine A has:`, ch:[`Less power`,`More power`,`The same power as Machine B`,`No power at all`], ans:1, exp:`Since power is work divided by time, doing the same work in less time means Machine A has greater power.` },
      { q:`One watt is equal to one joule of work done per:`, ch:[`Minute`,`Hour`,`Second`,`Meter`], ans:2, exp:`By definition, 1 watt = 1 joule per second.` },
      { q:`You hold a heavy book perfectly still out in front of you for one minute. According to the physics definition of work, how much work is done on the book?`, ch:[`A large amount, because your arm gets tired`,`Zero, because the book does not move`,`A small negative amount`,`It depends on the book's weight only`], ans:1, exp:`Since the book has no displacement while you hold it still, no work is done on it, even though your muscles use energy.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp05', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Potential & Kinetic Energy`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`Kinetic energy is the energy an object has because of its:`, ch:[`Position above the ground`,`Motion`,`Chemical makeup`,`Temperature only`], ans:1, exp:`Kinetic energy is the energy of motion — any moving object has kinetic energy.` },
      { q:`Gravitational potential energy depends on an object's:`, ch:[`Speed and color`,`Mass, height, and gravity`,`Temperature only`,`Electric charge`], ans:1, exp:`Gravitational potential energy = mass times gravity times height (PE = mgh), so it depends on how much mass is lifted and how high.` },
      { q:`As a ball falls from a high shelf toward the floor, its gravitational potential energy is converted into:`, ch:[`Kinetic energy`,`Electrical energy`,`Chemical energy`,`Nuclear energy`], ans:0, exp:`As the ball falls and loses height, its potential energy converts into kinetic energy as it speeds up.` },
      { q:`At the very top of a playground swing's arc, where it momentarily stops before swinging back, its energy is mostly:`, ch:[`Kinetic energy`,`Potential energy`,`Sound energy`,`Electrical energy`], ans:1, exp:`At the highest point, the swing briefly has zero velocity, so its kinetic energy is momentarily zero and its energy is almost entirely potential.` },
      { q:`If the velocity of a moving object doubles while its mass stays the same, its kinetic energy:`, ch:[`Doubles`,`Stays the same`,`Quadruples (4 times as much)`,`Is cut in half`], ans:2, exp:`Kinetic energy depends on velocity squared (KE = half times mass times velocity squared), so doubling velocity makes KE four times larger.` },
      { q:`If the mass of a moving object doubles while its velocity stays the same, its kinetic energy:`, ch:[`Doubles`,`Quadruples`,`Stays the same`,`Is cut in half`], ans:0, exp:`Kinetic energy is directly proportional to mass, so doubling the mass simply doubles the kinetic energy.` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for both kinetic and potential energy?`, ch:[`Watts`,`Newtons`,`Joules`,`Meters`], ans:2, exp:`Both kinetic and potential energy, like all forms of energy, are measured in joules (J).` },
      { q:`A roller coaster car at the very top of the first (tallest) hill has:`, ch:[`Maximum kinetic energy`,`Maximum gravitational potential energy`,`Zero total energy`,`Maximum sound energy`], ans:1, exp:`At the tallest point, the car is highest above the ground and moving slowest, so its gravitational potential energy is at its maximum.` },
      { q:`Which of these is an example of elastic potential energy?`, ch:[`A rock sitting on a cliff edge`,`A stretched rubber band`,`A moving car`,`Sunlight hitting a solar panel`], ans:1, exp:`Elastic potential energy is stored in stretched or compressed objects, like a stretched rubber band or a compressed spring.` },
      { q:`A 2 kg object moves at 3 m/s. What is its kinetic energy? (KE = half times mass times velocity squared)`, ch:[`6 J`,`3 J`,`9 J`,`18 J`], ans:2, exp:`KE = 0.5 times 2 kg times (3 m/s) squared = 0.5 times 2 times 9 = 9 J.` },
      { q:`A 5 kg object is lifted 2 meters (use g = 10 m/s squared). What is its gravitational potential energy?`, ch:[`10 J`,`50 J`,`100 J`,`20 J`], ans:2, exp:`PE = mass times gravity times height = 5 kg times 10 m/s squared times 2 m = 100 J.` },
      { q:`In an ideal system with no friction or air resistance, the total mechanical energy (kinetic plus potential) of an object:`, ch:[`Constantly increases`,`Constantly decreases`,`Stays constant (is conserved)`,`Drops to zero`], ans:2, exp:`Without energy losses to friction or air resistance, total mechanical energy is conserved — it simply shifts between kinetic and potential forms.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp06', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Momentum Basics`, diff:'Medium', qs:[
      { q:`Momentum is calculated using which formula?`, ch:[`p = mass times velocity`,`p = force times time`,`p = mass divided by velocity`,`p = mass times acceleration`], ans:0, exp:`Momentum (p) equals mass times velocity (p = mv).` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for momentum?`, ch:[`Joules`,`Watts`,`kg times m/s`,`Newtons per second`], ans:2, exp:`Momentum is measured in kilogram-meters per second (kg m/s), since it comes from mass (kg) times velocity (m/s).` },
      { q:`Momentum is considered a vector quantity because it has:`, ch:[`Only a numerical size`,`Both magnitude and direction`,`No physical meaning`,`Only a direction, no size`], ans:1, exp:`Since momentum depends on velocity, which is a vector, momentum also has both a size and a direction.` },
      { q:`The law of conservation of momentum states that in a closed system:`, ch:[`Momentum is always destroyed in collisions`,`Total momentum before a collision equals total momentum after`,`Only kinetic energy is conserved`,`Momentum only applies to objects at rest`], ans:1, exp:`As long as no outside forces act on the system, the total momentum before a collision equals the total momentum after it.` },
      { q:`A 2 kg cart moves at 5 m/s. What is its momentum?`, ch:[`2.5 kg m/s`,`7 kg m/s`,`10 kg m/s`,`25 kg m/s`], ans:2, exp:`Momentum = mass times velocity = 2 kg times 5 m/s = 10 kg m/s.` },
      { q:`In a perfectly elastic collision, which quantity is conserved along with momentum?`, ch:[`Kinetic energy`,`Mass alone changes`,`Only heat energy`,`Nothing else is conserved`], ans:0, exp:`In an elastic collision, both momentum and total kinetic energy are conserved, meaning no energy is lost to heat or sound.` },
      { q:`Two objects collide and stick together, forming one object. This type of collision is called:`, ch:[`Elastic`,`Perfectly inelastic`,`Frictionless`,`Impossible`], ans:1, exp:`When objects stick together after colliding, it is called a perfectly inelastic collision. Momentum is still conserved, but kinetic energy is not.` },
      { q:`Impulse is defined as force multiplied by time, and is also equal to:`, ch:[`The change in an object's momentum`,`The change in an object's mass`,`The object's total energy`,`The object's average speed`], ans:0, exp:`The impulse-momentum theorem states that impulse (force times time) equals the change in an object's momentum.` },
      { q:`A truck and a small car travel at the exact same speed. Compared to the car, the truck has:`, ch:[`Less momentum`,`The same momentum`,`More momentum`,`No momentum at all`], ans:2, exp:`Since momentum depends on mass, and the truck has much more mass than the car at the same speed, the truck has more momentum.` },
      { q:`Airbags reduce injury in a car crash mainly by:`, ch:[`Increasing the force of the collision`,`Increasing the time over which the passenger stops, reducing the force`,`Decreasing the passenger's momentum before the crash`,`Making the car heavier`], ans:1, exp:`By stretching out the time it takes a passenger to stop, an airbag reduces the force experienced for the same change in momentum.` },
      { q:`Two identical carts of equal mass move toward each other at the same speed and collide, sticking together. What is their final velocity?`, ch:[`Double the original speed`,`The same as the original speed`,`Zero`,`Half the original speed, in the original direction`], ans:2, exp:`The two equal and opposite momenta cancel out, so the combined object ends up with zero total momentum and zero velocity.` },
      { q:`A rocket expels exhaust gas backward at high speed, causing the rocket to accelerate forward. This is best explained by:`, ch:[`Conservation of mass`,`Conservation of momentum`,`Newton's Second Law alone`,`Gravity`], ans:1, exp:`The backward momentum of the expelled gas is balanced by an equal and opposite forward momentum gained by the rocket, keeping total momentum conserved.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp07', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Waves: Basic Properties`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`Wavelength is best described as the distance between:`, ch:[`A wave's source and an observer`,`Two consecutive crests (or any two matching points) of a wave`,`The top and bottom of a single crest`,`Two different waves`], ans:1, exp:`Wavelength is the distance from one point on a wave to the identical point on the next wave cycle, such as crest to crest.` },
      { q:`Frequency describes:`, ch:[`How tall a wave is`,`How many wave cycles pass a point each second`,`The speed of light only`,`The distance a wave travels in total`], ans:1, exp:`Frequency is the number of complete wave cycles that pass a given point in one second.` },
      { q:`Amplitude of a wave is most closely related to a wave's:`, ch:[`Speed`,`Energy`,`Frequency only`,`Wavelength only`], ans:1, exp:`Amplitude is the maximum height of a wave from its resting position, and larger amplitude generally means the wave carries more energy.` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for frequency?`, ch:[`Meters`,`Seconds`,`Hertz`,`Newtons`], ans:2, exp:`Frequency is measured in hertz (Hz), where 1 Hz equals one wave cycle per second.` },
      { q:`Which equation correctly relates wave speed (v), frequency (f), and wavelength (lambda)?`, ch:[`v = f + lambda`,`v = f times lambda`,`v = f divided by lambda`,`v = f minus lambda`], ans:1, exp:`Wave speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength (v = f times lambda).` },
      { q:`If wave speed stays constant, a wave with a higher frequency will have a:`, ch:[`Longer wavelength`,`Shorter wavelength`,`Larger amplitude`,`Smaller amplitude`], ans:1, exp:`Since v = f times lambda, if speed is fixed, frequency and wavelength are inversely related — higher frequency means shorter wavelength.` },
      { q:`In a transverse wave, the particles of the medium move:`, ch:[`Parallel to the direction the wave travels`,`Perpendicular to the direction the wave travels`,`In circles only`,`They do not move at all`], ans:1, exp:`In a transverse wave, such as a wave on a string or light wave, the medium vibrates at right angles to the direction the wave moves.` },
      { q:`In a longitudinal wave, the particles of the medium move:`, ch:[`Perpendicular to the direction the wave travels`,`Parallel to the direction the wave travels (back and forth)`,`Only upward`,`In a fixed circle`], ans:1, exp:`In a longitudinal wave, such as sound, particles vibrate back and forth along the same direction the wave is traveling.` },
      { q:`A wave has a frequency of 2 Hz and a wavelength of 3 m. What is its speed?`, ch:[`1.5 m/s`,`5 m/s`,`6 m/s`,`0.67 m/s`], ans:2, exp:`Wave speed = frequency times wavelength = 2 Hz times 3 m = 6 m/s.` },
      { q:`Increasing the amplitude of a wave, without changing its frequency, increases the wave's:`, ch:[`Wavelength`,`Speed in vacuum`,`Energy`,`Frequency`], ans:2, exp:`A larger amplitude means the wave is carrying more energy, even though its frequency and speed do not have to change.` },
      { q:`Which of the following is an example of a transverse wave?`, ch:[`A sound wave in air`,`A wave traveling along a shaken rope`,`A compression wave in a spring`,`A pressure wave in water pipes`], ans:1, exp:`A wave on a shaken rope moves up and down (perpendicular to its travel direction), making it a transverse wave, like light waves.` },
      { q:`Which of the following is an example of a longitudinal wave?`, ch:[`A sound wave traveling through air`,`Light traveling through space`,`A wave on the surface of the ocean`,`A wave on a guitar string`], ans:0, exp:`Sound waves are longitudinal waves because air particles compress and expand back and forth in the same direction the wave travels.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp08', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Sound Waves Basics`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`Sound is what type of wave?`, ch:[`Transverse`,`Longitudinal`,`Electromagnetic`,`Standing still`], ans:1, exp:`Sound is a longitudinal wave, made of compressions and rarefactions of the particles in the medium it travels through.` },
      { q:`Sound cannot travel through:`, ch:[`Air`,`Water`,`Steel`,`A vacuum (empty space)`], ans:3, exp:`Sound needs a medium (particles) to travel through by compressing and expanding them, so it cannot travel through the vacuum of space.` },
      { q:`Sound generally travels fastest through:`, ch:[`A vacuum`,`Gases like air`,`Liquids like water`,`Solids like steel`], ans:3, exp:`Sound travels fastest through solids because their particles are packed tightly together and can transmit vibrations quickly.` },
      { q:`The pitch of a sound is most closely related to its:`, ch:[`Amplitude`,`Frequency`,`Speed`,`Wavelength only, not frequency`], ans:1, exp:`Higher frequency sound waves are perceived as higher pitch, while lower frequency waves sound like a lower pitch.` },
      { q:`The loudness of a sound is most closely related to its:`, ch:[`Frequency`,`Wavelength`,`Amplitude`,`Speed`], ans:2, exp:`Loudness depends on the amplitude of the sound wave — a larger amplitude carries more energy and sounds louder.` },
      { q:`The Doppler effect explains why:`, ch:[`Sound cannot travel through water`,`A siren's pitch sounds different as it approaches versus moves away from you`,`Loud sounds travel farther than quiet ones`,`Echoes only happen indoors`], ans:1, exp:`The Doppler effect is the change in perceived frequency (pitch) of a sound caused by relative motion between the source and the listener.` },
      { q:`As an ambulance with its siren on drives toward you, the pitch you hear:`, ch:[`Sounds higher than the siren's actual pitch`,`Sounds lower than the siren's actual pitch`,`Stays exactly the same`,`Disappears completely`], ans:0, exp:`As the source moves toward you, sound waves bunch up in front of it, increasing the frequency you hear — this is the Doppler effect.` },
      { q:`An echo is caused by:`, ch:[`Sound waves speeding up`,`Sound waves reflecting off a surface`,`Sound waves losing their frequency`,`Sound turning into light`], ans:1, exp:`An echo happens when sound waves bounce (reflect) off a hard surface, like a wall or canyon, and return to your ears.` },
      { q:`The typical range of human hearing is approximately:`, ch:[`0 to 10 Hz`,`20 Hz to 20,000 Hz`,`1,000,000 to 2,000,000 Hz`,`200 Hz only`], ans:1, exp:`Most humans can hear sound frequencies roughly between 20 Hz (very low pitch) and 20,000 Hz (very high pitch).` },
      { q:`The speed of sound in air at room temperature is approximately:`, ch:[`3 m/s`,`34 m/s`,`343 m/s`,`3,000,000 m/s`], ans:2, exp:`Sound travels at about 343 m/s in air at room temperature, much slower than the speed of light.` },
      { q:`Ultrasound, used in medical imaging, refers to sound waves with frequencies:`, ch:[`Below human hearing range (under 20 Hz)`,`Above human hearing range (over 20,000 Hz)`,`Exactly at 440 Hz`,`That travel only through vacuum`], ans:1, exp:`Ultrasound waves have frequencies higher than 20,000 Hz, above what the human ear can detect.` },
      { q:`When sound travels from air into water, its speed:`, ch:[`Decreases`,`Increases`,`Stays exactly the same`,`Becomes zero`], ans:1, exp:`Sound actually travels faster in water than in air because water's particles are closer together and transmit vibrations more efficiently.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp09', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Light & Optics Basics`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`Light travels fastest through:`, ch:[`Water`,`Glass`,`A vacuum (empty space)`,`Diamond`], ans:2, exp:`Light travels fastest in a vacuum, where there is no matter to slow it down. It slows slightly when passing through materials like water or glass.` },
      { q:`The law of reflection states that the angle of incidence:`, ch:[`Is always greater than the angle of reflection`,`Equals the angle of reflection`,`Is always 90 degrees`,`Has no relationship to the angle of reflection`], ans:1, exp:`When light reflects off a surface, the angle at which it hits the surface equals the angle at which it bounces off.` },
      { q:`Refraction refers to:`, ch:[`Light bouncing directly backward`,`Light bending as it passes between materials of different densities`,`Light disappearing completely`,`Light traveling in a perfect vacuum`], ans:1, exp:`Refraction is the bending of light as it moves from one transparent material into another with a different optical density, such as air into water.` },
      { q:`A convex lens, like a magnifying glass, is also called a converging lens because it:`, ch:[`Spreads light rays apart`,`Bends light rays so they meet at a point`,`Blocks all light`,`Reflects all incoming light`], ans:1, exp:`A convex (converging) lens bends parallel light rays inward so they meet, or converge, at a focal point.` },
      { q:`A concave lens is also known as a diverging lens because it:`, ch:[`Focuses light to a single point`,`Spreads light rays apart`,`Reflects all light`,`Absorbs all light`], ans:1, exp:`A concave (diverging) lens causes parallel light rays to spread out (diverge) rather than meet at a point.` },
      { q:`A concave mirror, often used in makeup mirrors and telescopes, tends to:`, ch:[`Diverge (spread out) light rays`,`Converge (focus) light rays`,`Only absorb light`,`Have no effect on light`], ans:1, exp:`A concave mirror curves inward and focuses parallel light rays toward a single point, which is useful for magnifying images.` },
      { q:`When white light passes through a glass prism, it separates into a rainbow of colors. This process is called:`, ch:[`Reflection`,`Absorption`,`Dispersion`,`Polarization`], ans:2, exp:`Dispersion occurs because different colors (wavelengths) of light bend by slightly different amounts as they pass through the prism.` },
      { q:`The primary colors of light (used in mixing light, like on a screen) are:`, ch:[`Red, yellow, blue`,`Red, green, blue`,`Cyan, magenta, yellow`,`Black, white, gray`], ans:1, exp:`In light (as opposed to paint), the primary colors are red, green, and blue, which combine to make white light.` },
      { q:`The sky appears blue during the day mainly because:`, ch:[`The atmosphere absorbs all colors except blue`,`Shorter wavelengths of light, like blue, are scattered more by the atmosphere`,`The sun only emits blue light`,`Blue light travels slower than other colors`], ans:1, exp:`Blue light has a shorter wavelength and gets scattered in many directions by molecules in the atmosphere more than other colors, making the sky look blue.` },
      { q:`A real image, unlike a virtual image, can be:`, ch:[`Projected onto a screen`,`Only seen in a mirror`,`Only formed by concave lenses`,`Larger than the object at all times`], ans:0, exp:`A real image is formed by light rays that actually converge at a point, so it can be captured or projected onto a screen, unlike a virtual image.` },
      { q:`The approximate speed of light in a vacuum is:`, ch:[`300 m/s`,`3,000 m/s`,`300,000,000 m/s`,`3,000,000,000,000 m/s`], ans:2, exp:`Light travels at approximately 3 times 10 to the 8th power meters per second (300,000,000 m/s) in a vacuum.` },
      { q:`Total internal reflection, used in fiber optic cables, happens when light traveling in a denser medium hits a boundary at too steep an angle and:`, ch:[`Passes through completely unchanged`,`Reflects entirely back into the denser medium`,`Turns into sound`,`Stops moving completely`], ans:1, exp:`When light hits the boundary at an angle steeper than the critical angle, it reflects entirely back inside the denser medium instead of exiting, which fiber optic cables use to guide light.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp10', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Electricity Basics: Circuits & Current`, diff:'Medium', qs:[
      { q:`Electric current is best described as:`, ch:[`The buildup of charge on an object`,`The flow of electric charge over time`,`A type of magnetic field`,`The resistance of a wire`], ans:1, exp:`Electric current is the flow of electric charge, usually carried by electrons, through a conductor over time.` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for electric current?`, ch:[`Volts`,`Ohms`,`Amperes`,`Watts`], ans:2, exp:`Current is measured in amperes, often shortened to amps (A).` },
      { q:`Ohm's Law is written as:`, ch:[`V = I times R`,`V = I plus R`,`V = I divided by R`,`V = R divided by I`], ans:0, exp:`Ohm's Law states that voltage equals current times resistance (V = IR).` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for voltage?`, ch:[`Amperes`,`Ohms`,`Watts`,`Volts`], ans:3, exp:`Voltage, also called electric potential difference, is measured in volts (V).` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for electrical resistance?`, ch:[`Volts`,`Ohms`,`Amperes`,`Watts`], ans:1, exp:`Resistance is measured in ohms, often written with the symbol for the Greek letter omega.` },
      { q:`In a series circuit with multiple components, the current:`, ch:[`Is different through each component`,`Is the same through every component, since there is only one path`,`Only flows through one component at a time`,`Cannot be measured`], ans:1, exp:`In a series circuit, all components share a single path, so the same current flows through each one.` },
      { q:`In a parallel circuit, each branch:`, ch:[`Must have the same current as every other branch`,`Has the same voltage across it as the other branches`,`Has no current at all`,`Cannot connect to a shared power source`], ans:1, exp:`In a parallel circuit, each branch connects directly across the same two points, so each branch experiences the same voltage.` },
      { q:`A circuit has a voltage of 12 V and a resistance of 4 ohms. What is the current?`, ch:[`48 A`,`8 A`,`3 A`,`16 A`], ans:2, exp:`Using Ohm's Law, I = V / R = 12 V / 4 ohms = 3 A.` },
      { q:`Which of these materials is the best electrical conductor?`, ch:[`Rubber`,`Wood`,`Copper`,`Plastic`], ans:2, exp:`Copper is a metal with electrons that move freely, making it an excellent conductor of electricity, which is why it's used in wiring.` },
      { q:`Which of these materials is a good electrical insulator?`, ch:[`Copper`,`Rubber`,`Aluminum`,`Iron`], ans:1, exp:`Rubber does not allow electric charge to flow easily through it, making it a good insulator, often used to coat wires.` },
      { q:`If one light bulb burns out in a simple series circuit of bulbs, what happens to the other bulbs?`, ch:[`They stay lit as normal`,`They get brighter`,`They also go out, since the circuit is broken`,`They start blinking`], ans:2, exp:`In a series circuit, there is only one path for current, so if it breaks anywhere (like a burnt-out bulb), no current flows and all the bulbs go out.` },
      { q:`If one light bulb burns out in a parallel circuit of bulbs, what happens to the other bulbs?`, ch:[`They also go out immediately`,`They stay lit, since other paths for current still exist`,`The whole circuit stops working`,`They become insulators`], ans:1, exp:`In a parallel circuit, each bulb has its own separate path, so if one bulb burns out, current can still flow through the other paths and the rest stay lit.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp11', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Magnetism Basics`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`When two magnets have their like poles (such as north to north) facing each other, they will:`, ch:[`Attract each other`,`Repel each other`,`Have no effect on each other`,`Merge into one pole`], ans:1, exp:`Like magnetic poles (north-north or south-south) repel, or push away from, each other.` },
      { q:`When two magnets have opposite poles (north facing south) facing each other, they will:`, ch:[`Repel each other`,`Attract each other`,`Have no effect on each other`,`Cancel out and lose magnetism`], ans:1, exp:`Opposite magnetic poles (north-south) attract, or pull toward, each other.` },
      { q:`Outside a magnet, magnetic field lines point from the:`, ch:[`South pole to the north pole`,`North pole to the south pole`,`Center of the magnet outward in all directions equally`,`Magnet toward the observer only`], ans:1, exp:`By convention, magnetic field lines outside a magnet travel from the north pole around to the south pole.` },
      { q:`Which group of materials is strongly attracted to magnets?`, ch:[`Copper, aluminum, and gold`,`Iron, nickel, and cobalt`,`Wood, plastic, and glass`,`Water, oil, and air`], ans:1, exp:`Iron, nickel, and cobalt are ferromagnetic materials that are strongly attracted to magnets.` },
      { q:`A compass needle lines up with Earth's magnetic field, with its north-seeking end pointing roughly toward Earth's:`, ch:[`Geographic north`,`Center`,`Equator`,`Geographic south only`], ans:0, exp:`A compass needle's north end points roughly toward Earth's geographic north, because Earth itself acts like a giant magnet.` },
      { q:`An electromagnet is created by:`, ch:[`Rubbing two magnets together`,`Passing electric current through a coil of wire`,`Heating a piece of iron`,`Freezing a piece of steel`], ans:1, exp:`Running an electric current through a coiled wire (often wrapped around an iron core) generates a magnetic field, creating an electromagnet.` },
      { q:`Increasing the amount of current flowing through an electromagnet's coil will generally:`, ch:[`Decrease its magnetic strength`,`Increase its magnetic strength`,`Have no effect on its strength`,`Turn it into a permanent magnet`], ans:1, exp:`A stronger current flowing through the coil produces a stronger magnetic field, increasing the electromagnet's strength.` },
      { q:`A moving electric charge always creates:`, ch:[`A gravitational field`,`A magnetic field`,`Sound waves`,`Heat only`], ans:1, exp:`Moving electric charges, such as current flowing through a wire, generate a magnetic field around them.` },
      { q:`If you cut a bar magnet in half, you get:`, ch:[`One piece with a north pole and one with a south pole, and no magnetism otherwise`,`Two separate magnets, each with its own north and south pole`,`A single pole with no magnetism`,`Two pieces that are no longer magnetic at all`], ans:1, exp:`Magnetic poles cannot be separated — cutting a magnet in half creates two smaller magnets, each with both a north and a south pole.` },
      { q:`Which of these materials is NOT noticeably attracted to a common magnet?`, ch:[`Iron`,`Nickel`,`Cobalt`,`Copper`], ans:3, exp:`Copper is not a ferromagnetic material, so it is not noticeably attracted to ordinary magnets, unlike iron, nickel, and cobalt.` },
      { q:`An electric generator works by using magnetism to convert:`, ch:[`Electrical energy into light energy`,`Mechanical (motion) energy into electrical energy`,`Chemical energy into heat energy`,`Sound energy into electrical energy`], ans:1, exp:`Generators use magnets and coils of wire to convert mechanical motion, such as a spinning turbine, into electrical energy.` },
      { q:`An electric motor works by using magnetism to convert:`, ch:[`Electrical energy into mechanical (motion) energy`,`Mechanical energy into chemical energy`,`Light energy into sound energy`,`Heat energy into electrical energy`], ans:0, exp:`Motors use magnetic forces on current-carrying wires to convert electrical energy into mechanical motion, spinning a shaft.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp12', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Simple Machines`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`A simple machine makes work easier mainly by:`, ch:[`Increasing the total energy put into a task`,`Changing the amount of force needed, often by trading off distance`,`Eliminating the need for any force`,`Creating new energy`], ans:1, exp:`Simple machines let you apply less force over a longer distance (or vice versa) to accomplish the same amount of work.` },
      { q:`Which of these is a classic example of a lever?`, ch:[`A ramp`,`A seesaw`,`A pulley wheel`,`A screw`], ans:1, exp:`A seesaw is a simple lever, with a fulcrum (pivot point) in the middle and a load and effort on either side.` },
      { q:`Mechanical advantage describes:`, ch:[`How much a machine multiplies the input force`,`How much energy a machine wastes`,`The total mass of a machine`,`How fast a machine operates`], ans:0, exp:`Mechanical advantage is the ratio of output force to input force, showing how much a machine multiplies the effort you apply.` },
      { q:`On a seesaw (a first-class lever), the fixed pivot point is called the:`, ch:[`Load`,`Effort`,`Fulcrum`,`Axle`], ans:2, exp:`The fulcrum is the fixed point that a first-class lever, like a seesaw, pivots around, located between the effort and the load.` },
      { q:`An inclined plane (ramp) makes it easier to lift a heavy object by:`, ch:[`Reducing the total work needed`,`Increasing the distance traveled while decreasing the force needed`,`Eliminating friction completely`,`Making the object lighter`], ans:1, exp:`A ramp spreads the same amount of work over a longer distance, which reduces the force needed at any given moment.` },
      { q:`A pulley is most useful for:`, ch:[`Changing the direction of a force (and multiplying force with multiple pulleys)`,`Increasing an object's mass`,`Generating electricity`,`Producing heat`], ans:0, exp:`A single pulley changes the direction you need to pull, and a system of multiple pulleys can also multiply the force you apply.` },
      { q:`A doorknob and a steering wheel are both examples of which simple machine?`, ch:[`Inclined plane`,`Wedge`,`Wheel and axle`,`Screw`], ans:2, exp:`Both a doorknob and a steering wheel work as a wheel and axle, where turning the larger wheel rotates a smaller connected axle with greater force.` },
      { q:`An axe or a knife works mainly as which type of simple machine?`, ch:[`Lever`,`Wedge`,`Pulley`,`Wheel and axle`], ans:1, exp:`A wedge is a simple machine shaped to concentrate force into a point or edge, useful for splitting or cutting, like an axe blade.` },
      { q:`A screw can be thought of as which other simple machine wrapped around a cylinder?`, ch:[`Lever`,`Pulley`,`Wedge`,`Inclined plane`], ans:3, exp:`A screw is essentially an inclined plane (ramp) wound around a central cylinder, which is why turning a screw drives it forward with less force.` },
      { q:`Ignoring friction, a simple machine can never:`, ch:[`Change the direction of a force`,`Multiply the force applied to it`,`Produce more total work output than the work put into it`,`Make a task feel easier`], ans:2, exp:`Simple machines cannot create energy — they can only change how force and distance are traded off, never increasing total work output beyond what is put in.` },
      { q:`A worker applies 20 N of force to lift a 100 N load using a simple machine. What is the mechanical advantage?`, ch:[`2`,`5`,`20`,`100`], ans:1, exp:`Mechanical advantage = output force / input force = 100 N / 20 N = 5.` },
      { q:`Which simple machine is being used when movers push furniture up a ramp into a truck?`, ch:[`Wedge`,`Pulley`,`Inclined plane`,`Wheel and axle`], ans:2, exp:`A ramp is an inclined plane, which lets movers apply less force over a longer distance to lift furniture into the truck.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp13', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Gravity & Orbits Basics`, diff:'Medium', qs:[
      { q:`According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the gravitational force between two objects depends on:`, ch:[`Their colors and shapes`,`Their masses and the distance between them`,`Only their distance from the sun`,`Only their temperature`], ans:1, exp:`Gravitational force increases with the masses of the two objects and decreases as the distance between them increases.` },
      { q:`As the distance between two objects increases, the gravitational force between them:`, ch:[`Increases`,`Decreases`,`Stays exactly the same`,`Becomes negative`], ans:1, exp:`Gravitational force weakens as objects get farther apart, following an inverse relationship with distance.` },
      { q:`If the distance between two objects doubles, the gravitational force between them becomes:`, ch:[`Twice as strong`,`Half as strong`,`One-fourth as strong`,`Four times as strong`], ans:2, exp:`Gravity follows an inverse-square law, so doubling the distance reduces the force to one-fourth (1 divided by 2 squared) of its original strength.` },
      { q:`What is the key difference between weight and mass?`, ch:[`They are exactly the same thing`,`Weight is a force from gravity; mass is the amount of matter in an object`,`Mass changes depending on gravity; weight never changes`,`Weight is measured in kilograms; mass is measured in newtons`], ans:1, exp:`Mass is a fixed amount of matter in an object, while weight is the force of gravity acting on that mass, which can change depending on location.` },
      { q:`An astronaut who weighs 600 N on Earth would weigh less on the Moon because:`, ch:[`Their mass decreases on the Moon`,`The Moon's gravity is weaker than Earth's, even though their mass stays the same`,`They lose matter during the trip`,`The Moon has no gravity at all`], ans:1, exp:`The Moon's gravitational pull is weaker than Earth's, so the same mass experiences less gravitational force (less weight) on the Moon.` },
      { q:`Objects orbiting Earth, like satellites, are best described as:`, ch:[`Floating with no forces acting on them`,`Continuously falling toward Earth but moving forward fast enough to keep missing it`,`Held up by air pressure`,`Not affected by gravity at all`], ans:1, exp:`An orbiting object is constantly being pulled by gravity (falling) but also moving sideways fast enough that it continually falls around the Earth instead of hitting it.` },
      { q:`What force keeps the planets in orbit around the Sun?`, ch:[`Magnetism`,`Air pressure`,`The Sun's gravitational pull`,`Friction from space`], ans:2, exp:`The Sun's gravity constantly pulls on the planets, providing the centripetal force that keeps them moving in orbit rather than flying off in a straight line.` },
      { q:`The acceleration due to gravity near Earth's surface is approximately:`, ch:[`1 m/s squared`,`9.8 m/s squared`,`98 m/s squared`,`0.98 m/s squared`], ans:1, exp:`Near Earth's surface, gravity accelerates falling objects at approximately 9.8 m/s squared.` },
      { q:`If you drop a heavy ball and a light ball from the same height at the same time, with no air resistance, they will:`, ch:[`Hit the ground at the same time`,`The heavier ball hits first`,`The lighter ball hits first`,`Neither ball will fall`], ans:0, exp:`Without air resistance, all objects fall at the same rate regardless of mass, since gravitational acceleration does not depend on an object's mass.` },
      { q:`For an object moving in a circular orbit, the centripetal force needed to keep it on that path points:`, ch:[`Away from the center of the orbit`,`Toward the center of the orbit`,`In the same direction the object is moving`,`Upward, away from the ground`], ans:1, exp:`Centripetal force always points toward the center of the circular path, constantly redirecting the object's motion to keep it curving.` },
      { q:`If the mass of one of two objects is doubled while the distance between them stays the same, the gravitational force between them becomes:`, ch:[`Twice as strong`,`Half as strong`,`Four times as strong`,`Unchanged`], ans:0, exp:`Gravitational force is directly proportional to each object's mass, so doubling one mass doubles the force between them.` },
      { q:`Astronauts in the International Space Station appear weightless mainly because:`, ch:[`There is no gravity in space at that altitude`,`They and the station are in continuous free fall together around Earth`,`Their mass disappears in orbit`,`The station blocks Earth's gravity`], ans:1, exp:`Gravity is still present at that altitude — astronauts feel weightless because they and the station are both falling around Earth at the same rate, so there is no support force pushing back on them.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp14', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Heat & Thermal Energy Basics`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`Heat naturally flows from:`, ch:[`Cold objects to hot objects`,`Hot objects to cold objects`,`It does not flow, it just appears`,`Only from solids to liquids`], ans:1, exp:`Heat energy naturally moves from hotter objects to colder ones until they reach the same temperature (thermal equilibrium).` },
      { q:`Temperature is a measure of:`, ch:[`The total energy in an object`,`The average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance`,`How much an object weighs`,`The volume of an object`], ans:1, exp:`Temperature reflects how fast, on average, the particles in a substance are moving (their average kinetic energy).` },
      { q:`Conduction is the transfer of heat through:`, ch:[`Empty space with no matter`,`Direct contact between particles`,`Light waves only`,`Sound waves`], ans:1, exp:`Conduction transfers heat when particles in direct contact bump into each other, passing energy from faster to slower particles.` },
      { q:`Convection is the transfer of heat through:`, ch:[`The movement of fluid (liquid or gas) currents`,`Direct contact of solids`,`Electromagnetic waves through a vacuum`,`Sound vibrations`], ans:0, exp:`Convection occurs when warmer, less dense fluid rises and cooler, denser fluid sinks, creating currents that carry heat.` },
      { q:`Radiation transfers heat through:`, ch:[`Direct contact only`,`Fluid currents only`,`Electromagnetic waves, which can travel through empty space`,`Solid particles bumping into each other`], ans:2, exp:`Radiation transfers energy as electromagnetic waves, which is how heat from the Sun reaches Earth through the vacuum of space.` },
      { q:`Which method of heat transfer allows the Sun's energy to reach Earth through empty space?`, ch:[`Conduction`,`Convection`,`Radiation`,`Friction`], ans:2, exp:`Since space is a vacuum with no matter, only radiation (which does not need a medium) can carry heat energy from the Sun to Earth.` },
      { q:`A metal spoon left in hot soup becomes hot mainly due to:`, ch:[`Radiation`,`Conduction`,`Convection currents in the spoon`,`Evaporation`], ans:1, exp:`Heat travels through the solid metal spoon by conduction, as particles that are heated pass energy along to neighboring particles.` },
      { q:`Specific heat is best described as:`, ch:[`The temperature at which a substance boils`,`The amount of energy needed to raise a certain amount of a substance's temperature`,`The total energy stored in an object`,`The weight of a substance`], ans:1, exp:`Specific heat measures how much energy is required to raise the temperature of a given mass of a substance by a set amount, often one degree.` },
      { q:`Water has a relatively high specific heat, which means:`, ch:[`It takes a lot of energy to change its temperature`,`It heats up and cools down very quickly`,`It cannot store thermal energy`,`It boils at a very low temperature`], ans:0, exp:`Because water's specific heat is high, it absorbs or releases a large amount of energy before its temperature changes much, which helps regulate climate near large bodies of water.` },
      { q:`When most materials are heated, they tend to:`, ch:[`Shrink`,`Expand`,`Stay exactly the same size`,`Disappear`], ans:1, exp:`Heating typically causes particles to move more and spread out slightly, causing most materials to expand as their temperature rises.` },
      { q:`On the Fahrenheit scale, the freezing point of water (0 degrees Celsius) is:`, ch:[`0 degrees Fahrenheit`,`32 degrees Fahrenheit`,`100 degrees Fahrenheit`,`212 degrees Fahrenheit`], ans:1, exp:`Water freezes at 0 degrees Celsius, which corresponds to 32 degrees Fahrenheit.` },
      { q:`At sea level, water boils at approximately:`, ch:[`0 degrees Celsius`,`50 degrees Celsius`,`100 degrees Celsius`,`212 degrees Celsius`], ans:2, exp:`At standard atmospheric pressure (sea level), water boils at 100 degrees Celsius, which is 212 degrees Fahrenheit.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp15', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Density, Pressure & Buoyancy`, diff:'Medium', qs:[
      { q:`Density is calculated as:`, ch:[`Mass divided by volume`,`Volume divided by mass`,`Mass times volume`,`Mass plus volume`], ans:0, exp:`Density equals an object's mass divided by its volume, describing how tightly packed its matter is.` },
      { q:`What are common units for density?`, ch:[`Newtons`,`Watts`,`kg per cubic meter (or g per cubic centimeter)`,`Joules per second`], ans:2, exp:`Density is commonly expressed in kilograms per cubic meter or grams per cubic centimeter.` },
      { q:`An object that is less dense than water, when placed in water, will:`, ch:[`Sink to the bottom`,`Float`,`Dissolve`,`Explode`], ans:1, exp:`Objects less dense than the fluid they are placed in will float, because they weigh less than the fluid they displace.` },
      { q:`An object that is more dense than water, when placed in water, will:`, ch:[`Float on top`,`Sink`,`Stay suspended exactly in the middle`,`Turn into a gas`], ans:1, exp:`Objects denser than water sink because they weigh more than the water they displace.` },
      { q:`Pressure is calculated as:`, ch:[`Force divided by area`,`Force times area`,`Mass divided by volume`,`Force divided by time`], ans:0, exp:`Pressure equals the force applied divided by the area over which it is spread (P = F/A).` },
      { q:`What are the standard units for pressure?`, ch:[`Newtons`,`Joules`,`Pascals`,`Watts`], ans:2, exp:`Pressure is measured in pascals (Pa), where one pascal equals one newton of force per square meter.` },
      { q:`Archimedes' principle states that the buoyant force on a submerged object equals:`, ch:[`The object's total weight`,`The weight of the fluid displaced by the object`,`The volume of the object only`,`Zero, in all cases`], ans:1, exp:`Archimedes' principle says that the upward buoyant force on an object equals the weight of the fluid the object pushes out of the way (displaces).` },
      { q:`As you go deeper underwater, the water pressure around you:`, ch:[`Decreases`,`Increases`,`Stays exactly the same`,`Becomes negative`], ans:1, exp:`Water pressure increases with depth because there is more water (and its weight) pressing down from above.` },
      { q:`An object has a mass of 10 g and a volume of 2 cubic centimeters. What is its density?`, ch:[`20 g/cm3`,`0.2 g/cm3`,`5 g/cm3`,`12 g/cm3`], ans:2, exp:`Density = mass / volume = 10 g / 2 cm3 = 5 g/cm3.` },
      { q:`A large steel ship can float on water, even though steel itself is denser than water, mainly because:`, ch:[`Steel becomes less dense underwater`,`The ship's hollow shape displaces enough water that its overall average density is less than water`,`Ships have no weight at sea`,`Ocean water is denser than fresh water`], ans:1, exp:`The ship's hull shape encloses a lot of air, so the overall volume of the ship is large relative to its mass, giving it an average density less than water even though solid steel alone would sink.` },
      { q:`Ice floats on liquid water mainly because:`, ch:[`Ice is warmer than the water around it`,`Ice is less dense than liquid water`,`Ice has no mass`,`Water pushes ice upward with magnetism`], ans:1, exp:`Water is unusual in that its solid form (ice) is less dense than its liquid form, so ice floats instead of sinking.` },
      { q:`As altitude increases (going up a mountain, for example), atmospheric pressure:`, ch:[`Increases`,`Decreases`,`Stays exactly the same`,`Doubles every mile`], ans:1, exp:`There is less air (and less weight of air) pressing down above you at higher altitudes, so atmospheric pressure decreases as you go up.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp16', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`The Electromagnetic Spectrum`, diff:'Medium', qs:[
      { q:`Which type of electromagnetic wave has the longest wavelength?`, ch:[`Gamma rays`,`X-rays`,`Radio waves`,`Ultraviolet light`], ans:2, exp:`Radio waves have the longest wavelengths (and lowest frequencies) of all electromagnetic waves.` },
      { q:`Which type of electromagnetic wave has the shortest wavelength (and highest energy)?`, ch:[`Radio waves`,`Microwaves`,`Visible light`,`Gamma rays`], ans:3, exp:`Gamma rays have the shortest wavelengths and highest frequencies, giving them the most energy of all electromagnetic waves.` },
      { q:`In the visible light spectrum, which color has the longest wavelength?`, ch:[`Violet`,`Blue`,`Red`,`Green`], ans:2, exp:`Of the visible colors, red light has the longest wavelength, while violet has the shortest.` },
      { q:`Which type of electromagnetic wave is commonly used to see inside the body, such as imaging bones?`, ch:[`Radio waves`,`X-rays`,`Infrared light`,`Microwaves`], ans:1, exp:`X-rays can pass through soft tissue but are absorbed by denser material like bone, making them useful for medical imaging.` },
      { q:`Ultraviolet (UV) light from the Sun is best known for causing:`, ch:[`Sunburn`,`Radio static`,`Ocean tides`,`Earthquakes`], ans:0, exp:`UV light carries enough energy to damage skin cells, which is why overexposure to sunlight causes sunburn.` },
      { q:`Infrared radiation is most closely associated with:`, ch:[`Radio broadcasts`,`Heat, such as in thermal cameras and TV remote controls`,`Gamma radiation from nuclear reactions`,`Radar for weather forecasting`], ans:1, exp:`Infrared radiation is closely linked to heat, which is why thermal cameras detect infrared light and many remote controls use infrared signals.` },
      { q:`Microwaves are commonly used for:`, ch:[`Killing bacteria with high energy radiation`,`Cooking food and wireless communication`,`Producing visible light in light bulbs`,`Medical X-ray imaging`], ans:1, exp:`Microwaves are used in microwave ovens to heat food and in technologies like cell phones and radar for communication.` },
      { q:`In a vacuum, all types of electromagnetic waves travel at:`, ch:[`Different speeds depending on their color`,`The same speed, the speed of light`,`Speeds that depend on their energy`,`Zero speed`], ans:1, exp:`Regardless of wavelength or frequency, all electromagnetic waves travel at the same speed in a vacuum: the speed of light.` },
      { q:`As the frequency of an electromagnetic wave increases, its energy:`, ch:[`Decreases`,`Increases`,`Stays exactly the same`,`Becomes negative`], ans:1, exp:`Higher frequency electromagnetic waves carry more energy, which is why gamma rays and X-rays are more dangerous than radio waves.` },
      { q:`As the wavelength of an electromagnetic wave increases, its frequency:`, ch:[`Increases`,`Decreases`,`Stays exactly the same`,`Becomes zero`], ans:1, exp:`Wavelength and frequency are inversely related for electromagnetic waves — as wavelength gets longer, frequency gets lower.` },
      { q:`On the electromagnetic spectrum, visible light sits between which two types of radiation?`, ch:[`Gamma rays and X-rays`,`Radio waves and microwaves`,`Infrared and ultraviolet`,`X-rays and microwaves`], ans:2, exp:`Visible light occupies a narrow band between infrared (longer wavelength) and ultraviolet (shorter wavelength) radiation.` },
      { q:`Which of these lists electromagnetic waves correctly from longest to shortest wavelength?`, ch:[`Gamma rays, X-rays, visible light, radio waves`,`Radio waves, visible light, X-rays, gamma rays`,`Visible light, radio waves, gamma rays, X-rays`,`X-rays, gamma rays, radio waves, visible light`], ans:1, exp:`From longest to shortest wavelength, the order is: radio waves, then visible light, then X-rays, then gamma rays.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp17', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Energy Transformations & Conservation of Energy`, diff:'Medium', qs:[
      { q:`The law of conservation of energy states that energy:`, ch:[`Can be created but never destroyed`,`Can be destroyed but never created`,`Cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another`,`Constantly increases over time`], ans:2, exp:`Energy is never created or destroyed — it can only change from one form into another, such as from kinetic to potential energy.` },
      { q:`As a pendulum swings back and forth, its energy repeatedly transforms between:`, ch:[`Chemical and nuclear energy`,`Kinetic and potential energy`,`Electrical and magnetic energy`,`Sound and light energy`], ans:1, exp:`A swinging pendulum constantly converts kinetic energy (motion, at the bottom of the swing) into potential energy (height, at the top of the swing) and back.` },
      { q:`In a power plant with a generator, the main energy transformation is:`, ch:[`Electrical energy into chemical energy`,`Mechanical energy into electrical energy`,`Light energy into sound energy`,`Nuclear energy into magnetic energy directly, with no other steps`], ans:1, exp:`Generators use spinning turbines (mechanical motion) combined with magnets to produce electrical energy.` },
      { q:`A solar panel converts sunlight into:`, ch:[`Chemical energy for digestion`,`Sound energy`,`Electrical energy`,`Nuclear energy`], ans:2, exp:`Solar panels use light-sensitive materials to convert the energy from sunlight directly into electrical energy.` },
      { q:`When you eat food and then run, the chemical energy stored in the food is converted mainly into:`, ch:[`Nuclear energy`,`Mechanical (kinetic) energy and heat`,`Magnetic energy`,`Sound energy only`], ans:1, exp:`Your body breaks down chemical energy from food and converts it into the kinetic energy of movement, along with some heat.` },
      { q:`A battery is best described as a device that stores:`, ch:[`Mechanical energy`,`Chemical energy, which converts to electrical energy when used`,`Sound energy`,`Nuclear energy`], ans:1, exp:`Batteries store energy in chemical form and release it as electrical energy through a chemical reaction when connected in a circuit.` },
      { q:`When friction slows down a sliding object, the object's kinetic energy is mostly converted into:`, ch:[`Potential energy`,`Heat (thermal energy)`,`Nuclear energy`,`Magnetic energy`], ans:1, exp:`Friction converts kinetic energy into heat, which is why rubbing your hands together or sliding across a rough surface makes things warm.` },
      { q:`A bouncing ball eventually stops bouncing in the real world because:`, ch:[`Energy simply disappears, violating conservation of energy`,`Some of its energy is transformed into heat and sound with each bounce`,`Gravity turns off after enough bounces`,`The ball loses mass with each bounce`], ans:1, exp:`Each time the ball bounces, some of its mechanical energy converts into heat and sound due to friction and air resistance, so it bounces lower each time — the energy is transformed, not destroyed.` },
      { q:`A light bulb converts electrical energy mainly into:`, ch:[`Light and heat energy`,`Only sound energy`,`Only chemical energy`,`Nuclear energy`], ans:0, exp:`Light bulbs convert electrical energy into visible light, but also release some energy as heat, especially in older incandescent bulbs.` },
      { q:`A wind turbine converts the kinetic energy of moving air into:`, ch:[`Chemical energy`,`Electrical energy`,`Nuclear energy`,`Gravitational potential energy`], ans:1, exp:`Wind turbines use the kinetic energy of wind to spin blades connected to a generator, producing electrical energy.` },
      { q:`In a car's combustion engine, chemical energy stored in gasoline is mainly converted into:`, ch:[`Nuclear energy`,`Mechanical energy (and some heat)`,`Magnetic energy only`,`Sound energy only`], ans:1, exp:`Burning gasoline in the engine releases chemical energy, which is converted into mechanical energy to move the car, along with waste heat.` },
      { q:`On a frictionless roller coaster, the total mechanical energy (kinetic plus potential) at the top of the first hill compared to any other point on the track is:`, ch:[`Always greater at the top`,`Always less at the top`,`The same at every point, illustrating conservation of energy`,`Impossible to compare`], ans:2, exp:`Ignoring friction and air resistance, total mechanical energy stays constant throughout the ride — it just shifts between kinetic and potential forms.` },
    ]
  },
  { id:'hp18', cat:'Physical Sciences', title:`Basic Astronomy: The Solar System`, diff:'Easy', qs:[
      { q:`Which planet is closest to the Sun?`, ch:[`Venus`,`Earth`,`Mercury`,`Mars`], ans:2, exp:`Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun in our solar system.` },
      { q:`Which planet is commonly known as the "Red Planet"?`, ch:[`Venus`,`Mars`,`Jupiter`,`Saturn`], ans:1, exp:`Mars appears reddish because of iron oxide (rust) covering much of its surface, earning it the nickname "Red Planet."` },
      { q:`Which planet is the largest in our solar system?`, ch:[`Saturn`,`Neptune`,`Jupiter`,`Earth`], ans:2, exp:`Jupiter is by far the largest planet in the solar system, more massive than all the other planets combined.` },
      { q:`Which planet is most famous for its large, visible rings?`, ch:[`Mars`,`Saturn`,`Mercury`,`Earth`], ans:1, exp:`Saturn has the most prominent and easily visible ring system of any planet in the solar system, made mostly of ice and rock.` },
      { q:`What is the name of Earth's only natural satellite?`, ch:[`Titan`,`Europa`,`The Moon`,`Phobos`], ans:2, exp:`Earth has one natural satellite, simply called the Moon.` },
      { q:`Earth's seasons are caused primarily by:`, ch:[`Earth's changing distance from the Sun throughout the year`,`The tilt of Earth's axis as it orbits the Sun`,`The Moon blocking sunlight at certain times`,`Random changes in the Sun's brightness`], ans:1, exp:`Earth's axis is tilted about 23.5 degrees, so different parts of Earth receive more or less direct sunlight at different points in its orbit, creating seasons.` },
      { q:`One Earth year is defined as the time it takes Earth to:`, ch:[`Rotate once on its axis`,`Orbit once around the Sun`,`Orbit once around the Moon`,`Complete one day-night cycle`], ans:1, exp:`A year is the time it takes for Earth to complete one full orbit around the Sun, about 365.25 days.` },
      { q:`One Earth day is defined as the time it takes Earth to:`, ch:[`Orbit once around the Sun`,`Rotate once on its axis`,`Orbit once around the Moon`,`Travel one million miles`], ans:1, exp:`A day is the time it takes Earth to complete one full rotation on its axis, about 24 hours.` },
      { q:`What object sits at the center of our solar system?`, ch:[`Earth`,`The Moon`,`Jupiter`,`The Sun`], ans:3, exp:`The Sun sits at the center of the solar system, and all the planets orbit around it due to its strong gravitational pull.` },
      { q:`The asteroid belt is located between which two planets?`, ch:[`Earth and Mars`,`Mars and Jupiter`,`Jupiter and Saturn`,`Venus and Earth`], ans:1, exp:`The asteroid belt, a region full of rocky debris, lies between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter.` },
      { q:`Which is the smallest of the eight planets in our solar system?`, ch:[`Mars`,`Venus`,`Mercury`,`Earth`], ans:2, exp:`Mercury is the smallest of the eight planets, even smaller than some moons in the solar system, like Jupiter's Ganymede.` },
      { q:`The Sun is mostly made up of which two elements?`, ch:[`Oxygen and carbon`,`Hydrogen and helium`,`Iron and nickel`,`Nitrogen and neon`], ans:1, exp:`The Sun is a massive ball of mostly hydrogen and helium gas (plasma), with hydrogen fusing into helium at its core to produce energy.` },
    ]
  },
];
